      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{9}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx\\,dy.$",
      "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{9}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{9}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[\\,x\\,]_{{y}^{2}}^{9}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}(9-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{9}{y}-y)dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{9}{y}-y)dy & ={[9\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(9\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2})-(9\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =9-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(19-{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
